=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The HTTP server daemon on srv1 (10.5.0.4) — no web server process is running on the device that is supposed to serve all campus websites.

**Mechanism:** srv1 hosts the authoritative BIND DNS server for the `jeremysitlab.com` zone. The zone file contains a wildcard record (`* IN A 10.5.0.4`) so every campus hostname — including `srv1.jeremysitlab.com` and any other `*.jeremysitlab.com` address — resolves correctly to 10.5.0.4. DNS name resolution itself is fully functional and not the cause of the outage. The failure occurs one step later: when a client opens a TCP connection to 10.5.0.4 port 80, it is immediately refused because no HTTP daemon is listening. Curl returns code 000 with sub-5 ms latency, which is the fingerprint of an instant connection refusal, not a timeout or DNS miss.

**Localization:** Device `srv1`, IP `10.5.0.4`. `ps aux` shows only two processes: `bash` (PID 1) and `named` (BIND DNS, PID 30). `ss -tlnp` confirms port 53 is the only listener — port 80 is entirely absent. The localhost HTTP check from service_snapshot also returns code 000, ruling out any in-path ACL or routing cause.

**Root cause:** The web server process (nginx, Apache, or equivalent) was never started — or was stopped — on srv1, leaving port 80 with no listener while the DNS service continued operating normally. The user misidentified this as "DNS being down" because browsers present an HTTP connection refusal as "site can't be found."

**Fix:** Install (if missing) and start the appropriate HTTP server on srv1 (e.g., `nginx` or `apache2`), ensure it is configured to serve the campus website content, and enable it to start automatically on boot.
=== END ===